00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:05.540
JOHN BANNING: All right.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:10.760
I think we go back to the
slides now and here we go.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.680
We're going to go through the
query notation in seven steps.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.638
I'm not going to read
this to you because we're

00:08:15.638 --> 00:08:17.370
going to start right in.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.070
So basic query.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.770
The most basic query--

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.290
every query in
the query notation

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.700
starts with a fetch operation.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.580
The fetch operation,
we see one here,

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.230
starts with the name
of the operation,

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:34.010
fetch, and one argument,
which is the name of a table.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:38.240
When it evaluates, it's going to
find that table in the database

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.020
and return it or produce
it as it's output.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:45.410
Now, you might be
saying, wait a minute,

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.770
we have metrics, we have time
series, what's this table thing

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.250
and what's a table name?

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.050
So let's look at
that for a minute.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.100
Out in the cloud,
we've got resources

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.230
that we want to keep an eye on.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:58.910
Here's a Kubernetes node.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.055
And every kind of resource
has associated with it a--

00:09:03.055 --> 00:09:08.540
let's see, yes-- a monitored
resource type, in this case,

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:10.370
k8s_node.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.260
And then monitored
resource type gives us

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.470
a way to name that resource.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:17.600
And it does so by giving
us a set of labels.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.030
And every resource of that
kind will have a value

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.530
for each of those labels.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:24.770
The project ID that owns the
resource, the location where

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:27.695
the resource resides or
is being monitored from,

00:09:27.695 --> 00:09:29.570
and the cluster name
and node name that we're

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:32.990
given when that Kubernetes
is node was created,

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:37.370
and the monitor resource type,
and the value of those labels

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:42.490
uniquely identify every
Kubernetes node in the system.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.690
Now-- oops.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.670
Now, we're going to
have a number of metrics

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.670
on each one of these nodes that
we're going to be monitoring.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:51.650
And each metric
has a metric type.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.740
Here is a metric type for
allocatable utilization

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.080
on a node, the
fraction of memory

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.580
we could allocate that
we're actually using.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.210
And the metric type
gives the type of value

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:05.150
that the metric is producing,
in this case, double gauge.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:10.400
Gauge means that it's a state of
the system that we can observe

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.700
and that we'll sample,
to gather data from--

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.300
being the amount of memory
in use at that time.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.740
The other stream
that we see typically

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.060
is a delta stream,
where each point

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:27.950
is the sum of some value
like the number of queries

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:31.070
I've received or the
number of CPU seconds

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:36.090
I've used over some
specific time period.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.750
Now, the other thing that
might come with a metric type

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:39.560
are some labels.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:41.070
This one comes with
one label called

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.890
the memory type,
which can be either

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.650
a evictable or non-evictable.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.930
So there's two kinds of
memory and we'll, basically,

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.690
have two instances of this
metric with different values

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:54.620
for that label to track
the utilization of both

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.630
of those kinds of memory.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.860
Now, these two things
together uniquely edify

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:04.070
a stream of data that we might
be interested in collecting

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.040
and we put them together in
what's called a time series

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.840
identifier.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.610
The name of that time
series identifier

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:13.670
includes both the resource
name and the metric name,

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.000
has all of the labels from both
the resource and the metric,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.730
and this uniquely
identifies a stream

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:24.470
of data that's coming in
from the cloud to Stackdriver

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:25.890
to be recorded.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.710
Now, that we have a
name for a time series,

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.750
we can start collecting
at time series.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:32.600
So a time series has an
identifier that uniquely

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.520
identifies it and a history.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.810
So we get the history
and consists of values

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:42.620
that we've observed the metric
to have in the timestamp when

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:46.230
the observation was made.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:49.580
Now, obviously, we have a lot
of nodes out in the cloud,

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.500
and they have a lot
of metrics, and so

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.270
we're going to have a
lot of these time series.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.900
And you might think of this
slide as being reproduced--

00:11:56.900 --> 00:12:01.610
once for each resource and
metric type and they'll

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:05.090
have different labels, but the
time series same-- time series

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.840
identifier uniquely identifying
the time series and a history

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.860
associated with it.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:16.070
Now, in order to make these time
series accessible to queries,

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.430
we gather them together
in a time series table.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:23.270
This is a straightforward
relational table.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:26.660
It has columns, one column
for each of the times

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:30.250
series identifier labels,
a timestamp column--

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.446
whoops, sorry, there we go--

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:39.230
I went way too far, all right--

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:43.330
a timestamp column
and value column,

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:47.170
so every row in this table
represents one observation.

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:50.740
And that observation's time
is here and value there.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:55.630
And the time series it's part of
is identified by these columns.

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:57.880
So you can think of this as
containing all of the time

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:01.270
series that involve
[INAUDIBLE],, Kubernetes node,

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:06.850
and this particular metric.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:10.540
And so that's what the--

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:17.500
and the name of this table
is given by the resource

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:19.360
name and the metric name.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:25.420
And so that is what the
fetch operation names

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:30.130
and the kind of table
that it's going to return.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.600
All right.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.890
Now, let's start building
queries from that base.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:37.810
We build queries
with a pipe operator,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:43.030
that's the method by which
parts of queries are composed.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:47.920
Here the pipe operator that
connects different operations--

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.600
here we have the
fetch operation we

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.680
saw before connected by the pipe
operator to a filter operation

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.105
that we've added.

00:13:55.105 --> 00:13:56.980
And the way this works
is the fetch operation

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:01.810
produces a table, it's
piped by the pipe operator

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.750
to be the input to the
filter operation, which

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.880
transforms that
table in some way,

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.910
and produces it as
output, which is then

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:12.610
the output of this query.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:14.640
So we can see this
in this diagram.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:17.110
There's the fetch operation.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:20.800
It's producing a table
that's input to the filter

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.260
operation, which
transforms it in some way

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.240
and produces another
table as output.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.750
Well, what does the fetch
operation filter operation

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:30.070
doing?

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:32.660
Pretty straightforward,
it's got one argument,

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:36.670
which is a general express--
is a Boolean expression that's

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:37.750
a predicate.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:39.730
And we'll evaluate
that expression

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.190
on every row in the input table.

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:45.910
And those rows for which the
expression is true, we'll keep,

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.000
and those for which
is false, we'll drop.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.450
This is a fairly
simple expression.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:56.290
It's basically looking
at one column--

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.470
the value of one column
in the row and asking

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:03.040
does it start with
the word heapster?

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.180
If it does, it'll be true.

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:07.060
Otherwise, it'll be false.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:12.380
We'll see more complex
expressions in a little bit.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:15.580
So, basically, we're looking
at the container column

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:18.880
in all of the rows in the
table we're evaluating

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.520
the predicate on them.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:25.720
We see the rows that have
the predicate returning true.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.340
We throw away all the rows
where it returns false.

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:31.180
And this is the output
of the filter operation.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.180
So this is a way to look at
some specific subset of the data

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.582
that you want-- that
you have in your table.

00:15:37.582 --> 00:15:39.040
The other thing--
another thing you

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.860
might want to do is to get a
statistic about-- statistics

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:44.850
about all of the
things in the table

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:47.330
and that's done by the
group by operation.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:50.080
So here we're piping the output
of the fetch into a group

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:51.890
by, which has two arguments.

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:54.280
The first is a map
expression, which

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.380
is going to tell us how to
construct the time series

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:00.190
identifier columns
in the output table.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:03.490
And, here, they are constructed
by just taking the cluster name

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:09.430
field from the input
table stream ID

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:12.100
and that will be the
stream ID of the output.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:16.081
And then the other part
is the aggregation--

00:16:16.081 --> 00:16:19.630
I apologize, push on
the wrong buttons here--

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.430
the aggregation
expression, which

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.850
tells how to combine
the value columns

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:29.330
of rows that are mapped
to the same place by this.

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.980
So let's look at that
in a little more detail.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.800
Here we're showing one
stream out of the table.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:41.290
We have all of the stream ID
identifier column values here

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:44.200
and each row is represented
by a point in the table

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:46.220
with its timestamp.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:47.590
And, basically, what we do--

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:54.070
what the map does is, basically,
transform this into this.

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:56.230
And this is now the time
series of the identifier,

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:58.760
the history remains unchanged.

00:16:58.760 --> 00:17:02.440
Now, if we do that, we're going
to have a lot of time series

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:05.530
in the table with the same
time series identifier,

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:07.960
but we only want one
time series in the output

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.460
with that identifier.

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.740
So, basically, what
we're going to do

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.339
is to apply the
aggregator to these.

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:19.630
We take all of the points
at some particular time

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:21.670
and we apply the mean
aggregator to them,

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:23.650
getting the average
value, and that

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:27.550
goes into a point
in the output table

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:31.180
with the same timestamp
and the mean value.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:34.150
And we keep doing
that at each timestamp

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:37.840
until we get to the end of the
table, at which point, now,

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:42.990
we have one output time
series for the output table.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:48.020
We do this for every
value of cluster name,

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:49.780
so for every value
of cluster name,

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:52.600
there'll be another group
of time series like this

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:54.260
and we do the same thing.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:59.110
And that's how we
produce the output table.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:17:59.920
All right.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.095
So let's look at
that a little bit--

00:18:03.095 --> 00:18:03.595
in action.

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:09.010
We start with the basic fetch.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:13.240
We can-- and we can see there's
quite a few times series up

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.190
on the screen here.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:19.250
The-- and every
line on this screen

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:21.290
represents one time
series in the table

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:23.090
that was fetched by that fetch.

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:24.740
Now we can add a filter or this.

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.180
And what are we
going to do here?

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:36.250
We're going to look
at the namespace name.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.760
We're going to look at
the default namespace.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:44.270
And that's going to cut
down the rows that we have--

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:45.770
the [? street ?]
time series that we

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:49.410
have to a certain
smaller number of them.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.640
And we're also
going to look at--

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:57.510
we can add further
predicates to that.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.270
So we're going to only look--

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:00.860
some of these are
running on-prem,

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:04.660
we're only going to look
at the on-prem ones.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:05.620
All right.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:12.986
And once we've done that, now
we can look at a group by.

00:19:12.986 --> 00:19:18.060
And this group by is throwing
away all of the time series

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.580
identifiers, so there's only
one stream in the output

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:25.950
and it has the default
aggregator, which is to sum.

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:27.960
But we can replace
that aggregator

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:28.890
with an explicit one.

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:32.880
We could say mean or we
could say standard deviation.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.420
There's a very large number
of aggregators we could use.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.760
But besides giving a single
aggregation function,

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:39.870
we can make an expression.

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:43.140
We could, for instance,
divide the standard deviation

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:46.950
by the mean to get the
coefficient of variation

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.240
and that's what we have.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:53.280
So this is over all of the
containers in our application.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:57.480
Now, the next thing we can do
is go to the map expression.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:03.390
And here we're going to put
in this regular expression

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:07.140
extraction that extracts
the first component

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.430
in the name of the--

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.660
in the pod name
of our container--

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:16.650
everything up to the first dash.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:19.560
And so this gives us
aggregated information

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:25.050
about each kind of container
that we are running.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:28.810
All right.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:31.930
Now, after I've gone to all
this trouble to make a query,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.240
I might want to save it.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:36.380
Well, you can do that.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:37.345
There we go.

00:20:37.345 --> 00:20:38.560
You can save it.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:43.678
You give it a name and you
can save it to a dashboard.

00:20:43.678 --> 00:20:45.220
You could create a
new one or-- we're

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:48.280
going to use an existing
one and we save it.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:53.450
Think we save it?

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.870
Does it not want to save itself?

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.132
JD VELASQUEZ: [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:59.132 --> 00:21:01.430
JOHN BANNING: All right.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:02.747
JD VELASQUEZ: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:02.747 --> 00:21:04.580
JOHN BANNING: We're
going to try that again.

00:21:10.277 --> 00:21:11.860
JD VELASQUEZ: It's
the last day, John.

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:14.080
The demo gods are--

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:17.340
JOHN BANNING: They
are frowning on us?

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:18.180
All right.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:18.910
JD VELASQUEZ: We'll get to it--

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:20.080
JOHN BANNING: You're
going to have to trust us.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:21.090
You really can do this.

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:23.950
All right.

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:27.000
We would have gone to look
at the chart on the dashboard

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.277
and shown that you could
edit the chart again.

00:21:30.277 --> 00:21:32.610
In fact, we could go to the
dashboard and just edit some

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.610
other chart-- just to
show that it is, in fact--

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:35.550
JD VELASQUEZ: Yep.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:37.110
JOHN BANNING: --a useful thing.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.330
So this is also a
way of, obviously,

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.860
of creating dashboard.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:42.360
All right.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.790
Here's one we saved
earlier today, just

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.350
to prove that we can do it.

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:49.830
And you go back and you
get a very similar editor

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:51.580
view of that.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:52.080
All right.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:56.550
So let's go back now
to the presentation.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:03.490
So this is just for
the record, the group

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:06.370
by that we built before
with a little bit more here.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:09.400
We've got an extra field
that we added to the mapping,

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:13.430
but this is pretty
much the same thing.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:17.470
Now, let's see-- look at
some more advanced queries

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:18.100
from this.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:21.830
This is the base
queries that we've got.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:22.670
So here we go.

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:27.800
We're now up to point
five in our seven points.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:29.890
And one of the things
that you might want to do

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:31.270
is to look at outliers.

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:33.310
That's always a lot
of fun, particularly

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.730
if your aggregation says,
well, there's something wrong.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:37.480
Maybe you want to see
if there's something

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:39.520
wrong with specific things.

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:43.600
So for that you can
use the top operation.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:45.050
It has two arguments.

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:46.690
The first says,
how many things do

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:49.450
you want to see out of the
collection of all the things?

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:52.280
And the second says, here
is how to select them.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:54.130
So this is an
aggregation function,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:55.650
max is an aggregation function.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.080
It's applied to all of the
points in a time series

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:01.390
to get the maximum value
of that time series.

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:04.240
And then we look for the time
series, which the top five time

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:05.890
series for that value.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.010
You can use any kind
of aggregator here,

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:11.030
mean is also a good one to use.

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:14.680
And you can also use bottom
here to look for things

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.260
with the smallest amount.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:22.060
Another thing that you
often will want to do

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:24.100
is look at more than
one query at a time.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:26.710
So far, we've just been
looking at one query.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:31.480
Here we-- in order to do that,
you use these squiggly brackets

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:32.770
and join.

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:37.180
Between the squiggly brackets
you have multiple queries.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:39.970
Here are the queries are very
simple, just a fetch operation.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:43.750
But they could be general
queries put together by pipes,

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:48.050
separated by semicolons.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.380
So this, basically, produces
two different tables,

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:53.230
one for each of the queries.

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.170
Those two tables, then, are
piped into the join, which

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:57.940
combines them into one table.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:00.490
But that table now has
two value columns, one

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:02.590
from each of the input tables.

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:06.430
And that can be piped into a div
operation, which takes the two

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:08.560
values, takes the--

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:11.560
divides them, and
produces a single ratio,

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:14.840
and a single value
column table for output.

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:19.100
So let's look at that
in a little more detail.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:23.860
So here is how tables are piped
through the join operation.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:27.190
Each of the two queries
produces a table.

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:28.750
Those two tables
are independently

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:32.800
input into the join,
which combines the two

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:35.950
tables into one table
with two value columns.

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:38.560
The div takes that table
with two value columns,

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:40.360
divides the values
in the value column

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:43.390
to produce a table
with one value column.

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:46.690
So how does the join
do that transformation

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:49.240
from two tables to one?

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:50.810
Basically, it's two steps.

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:53.620
The first step, you're
looking at every pair of rows,

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:57.550
one from each of the
two tables, and asking

00:24:57.550 --> 00:25:00.640
do they match up in the
time series identifier

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.170
fields and the timestamp field?

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:04.510
And when they find two--

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.820
a pair that does, we
say we have a match

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:09.340
and we're going to combine
those two input rows

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.830
into a row in the output table.

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.410
And that's done
basically by taking

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:17.920
the time series
identifier columns

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:20.110
and the timestamp column,
which are the same in both

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:22.600
of those tables, and
those-- that-- those

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:25.480
become the column values
for that row in the output.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.990
And then taking the value column
from one table and value column

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:32.810
from the other, and those become
two value columns in the table.

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:37.900
And this is, basically, your
straightforward, relational

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:40.150
database natural join.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:46.810
Now, here we just did something
very simple with that table

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:47.890
with two values in it.

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:50.320
We just divided the two
values, but maybe you

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:52.630
want to do something
a little more complex.

00:25:52.630 --> 00:26:00.520
Here we're fetching the received
bytes and sent bytes for a pod

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.760
and we've joined that,
so now every row in the

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.850
has the number of received
bytes and the sent bytes.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:08.740
And we want the number
of received bytes

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.120
as a percent of the
total, so that's

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:13.840
this fairly
straightforward expression.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.103
Val 0 is the first
value in the table,

00:26:16.103 --> 00:26:17.270
which is the received bytes.

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:19.240
Val 1 is the second value.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:23.058
We could have used received byte
count and sent byte count here.

00:26:23.058 --> 00:26:24.850
It wouldn't have fit
on this slide as well,

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:28.280
but it would be maybe more
readable in some sense.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:29.420
We could have done that.

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:32.750
So that's the sum of
bytes sent and received.

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.750
Here's the bytes
received times 100

00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:37.220
and dividing we get
the percent of those.

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.210
So the value table
operation, basically,

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.850
allows us to create--

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:48.190
do arbitrary computations
on the input table.

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:53.650
The join can handle
multiple queries in it

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:57.130
and will join them all together.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:27:01.660
So you can look at more than
two metrics at the same time,

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:06.040
and you can create more than
one value column on the output.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:09.520
Unfortunately, at this time,
the metrics explorer will only

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.560
display the first
value in the column,

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:14.740
but you might have
additional computations that

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:16.430
could use multiple
values in the row

00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:22.680
and the value operation will be
quite happy to do that for you.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:24.110
All right.

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:25.747
More demoing-- all right.

00:27:25.747 --> 00:27:27.580
JD VELASQUEZ: Before
[INAUDIBLE] we actually

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:30.410
had some latency, which is
[INAUDIBLE] we [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:30.910
see here.

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:31.785
JOHN BANNING: Oh, OK.

00:27:31.785 --> 00:27:33.973
Are we going to save the chart?

00:27:33.973 --> 00:27:34.890
JD VELASQUEZ: Why not?

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:37.122
JOHN BANNING: Save the
chart, save the world.

00:27:37.122 --> 00:27:38.830
JD VELASQUEZ: Yeah,
let's see if it does.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:40.840
Actually did see a--

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:43.302
it's a latency
[INAUDIBLE] come back--

00:27:43.302 --> 00:27:45.010
JOHN BANNING: Let the
latency comes back?

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:46.720
JD VELASQUEZ: Save the chart.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:48.040
JOHN BANNING: I don't know, JD.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:49.170
We keep doing this,
they're not going

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:50.886
to believe that we
can actually do it.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:55.050
All right.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:55.835
Where are we now?

00:27:55.835 --> 00:27:56.405
JD VELASQUEZ: There you go.

00:27:56.405 --> 00:27:57.660
JOHN BANNING: We did alerting.

00:27:57.660 --> 00:27:58.350
Did we do alerting?

00:27:58.350 --> 00:27:58.860
No, we haven't done alerting.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:27:59.850
JD VELASQUEZ: Yeah, we
haven't done alerting.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:00.660
JOHN BANNING: All right.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:02.970
So one of the things you
might want to do with a query

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:04.740
is to set up an alert with it.

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.010
So JD is going to go to the
standard alert creation--

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:14.320
policy creation and we're
going to add the condition.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:16.740
And here we see an
editing window--

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.990
he's going to give the
condition a name-- there he is.

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:20.782
We see an-- our editing
window very similar

00:28:20.782 --> 00:28:28.320
to the one we saw before and
with the little editor thing

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:32.020
there and now we're back in
the query notation editor.

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:36.120
JD Is going to add a
query that, basically,

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:40.680
gives the mean utilization
of all of the containers

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.780
by location.

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:45.270
And we want to
alert if that gets

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:51.930
too high, so he's going to
put an alert operation there.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.340
The alert operation
has two arguments.

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:57.120
The first is a Boolean
expression, in this case,

00:28:57.120 --> 00:29:00.060
just checking a threshold,
but it could be arbitrary

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.255
and that when it's true,
says the condition is--

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:07.440
exists.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:10.170
And the second
argument is a duration

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:12.630
that says how long the
condition has to exist

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:14.850
before we fire an alert.

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:19.270
And having set that up,
we can now save it--

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:23.410
I hope-- there it
goes and so there we

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:26.483
see it in the alert condition.

00:29:26.483 --> 00:29:28.150
And the rest of it
is the standard thing

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:31.270
for just setting up alerts,
saying where you want it send,

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:33.190
some documentation.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:38.860
So we're going to go back
now to the metrics explorer.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:45.700
We saw a join that joined
two different metrics, now

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:48.040
we're going to
look at a join that

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:52.690
takes one table produced by
the fetch and the filter,

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:54.220
in this example--

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:58.450
that's one table-- and with
the squiggly brackets is going

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:02.380
to apply two continuation
queries to that--

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:08.140
one that's grouping by the kind
of pod, the container it's in,

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:10.570
and the other is grouping
everything together

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:12.130
and giving the total.

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:13.870
We join those two,
and we divide,

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:18.820
and that gives you the
fraction of core usage

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:23.560
time for each kind of pod.

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:25.210
And one nice thing
you can do with that

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:28.630
is to display it with
the stacked area graph

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:32.680
and that shows you a very nice
breakdown of where the CPU

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:35.670
time is going in all of these--

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:38.848
in all of your containers.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:45.290
Another thing that is something
that you would like to do

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:50.010
is to compare things between
now and sometime in the past.

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:52.730
So here we have a query--

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:57.710
again, we're modifying a table
that was produced by a fetch,

00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:00.400
and a filter, and
also a group by--

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:02.990
is all part of
producing a table.

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:06.620
This one gives us,
basically, the sum

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:10.860
of core usage time
for each container

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:16.050
name and we do two things to it.

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:17.350
First, we just add--

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.610
on one we just add a
time series identifier

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:22.428
label that says, when is now.

00:31:22.428 --> 00:31:24.470
And then the second one
we add a label that says,

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:25.730
when last week.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.340
And then we time shift that--

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:29.390
the table.

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:32.360
And the time shift,
basically, takes one argument

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:34.250
that says how far
are we shifting,

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:37.040
so we're shifting values
in the table from a week

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:39.030
ago to the present.

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:40.890
We're shifting them
forward in time.

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:43.970
So now we have a table for
now and a table for last week

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:46.260
that are both at the same time.

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:51.470
And you can see the tables
here, as JD switches between now

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.280
and last week-- you
can see the comparison

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:58.700
between what was going on
last week and next week.

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:03.038
Now, here we used the union
operation rather than the join

00:32:03.038 --> 00:32:05.330
to combine the multiple tables
because we wanted to see

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:08.310
both of these independently.

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:11.370
Another way to do this is
we could do a time shift.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:13.860
Here we are again-- time shift.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:15.710
One, we're just
changing the name

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:18.980
of the value in the
table and the other

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:21.830
we're shifting by a week
and here we do a join

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:24.050
and then do a division.

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:28.400
And now we see the ratio between
what was going on this week

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:30.890
and what was going on last week.

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:32.760
And some of this
looks highly erratic,

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:35.990
but that's because
this app is driven--

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:40.190
is being driven by robots and
we keep turning them on and off,

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:43.250
so it kind of looks like that.

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:45.050
All right, so time shift.

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:50.720
Another very nice thing
that Stackdriver has

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:55.640
is metadata, which
describes various aspects

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:57.690
of the resources.

00:32:57.690 --> 00:33:02.330
And here we have the metadata
node name for a container

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:05.780
and that, basically,
says what node--

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:09.950
what Kubernetes node is
that container running on.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.860
And so here we're
using that as a filter.

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:15.170
We have the name
of a node and we

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:18.590
want to get all of the
containers whose metadata says

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:20.330
I run on that node together.

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.300
And that's what this
filter is doing.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.320
And so now we can see,
for a particular node,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.210
what are all the containers
that are running on it.

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:30.780
So metadata is a
very nice thing.

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:32.570
It's useful in
filters now, it'll

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:35.420
be useful before
we get into beta

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:38.990
and a lot of other kinds of
expressions to group by them

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:42.800
or use them in
value expressions.

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:43.850
All right.

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:48.793
And one last thing is--

00:33:48.793 --> 00:33:50.960
let's see, oh, no, I've got
a couple of last things.

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:51.980
JD VELASQUEZ: Yeah.

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:55.490
JOHN BANNING: So
the next thing is--

00:33:55.490 --> 00:34:05.570
here is a query that, basically,
is got a lot of time series

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:06.205
in it.

00:34:06.205 --> 00:34:07.580
And one thing we
might want to do

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:11.000
is to cut that down by
doing a random selection.

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:13.940
And we can do that by
using the top table

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:18.889
operation with the hash time
series identifier operation.

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:21.679
So this is now going
to pick 10 streams

00:34:21.679 --> 00:34:25.699
at random by creating a
random hash of the time

00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:28.190
series for each--

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:30.860
time series identifier
for each time series

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:33.949
and then just picking the top
10 in that, which will give us

00:34:33.949 --> 00:34:35.300
a random selection.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.790
You can see, as JD
moves the cursor

00:34:37.790 --> 00:34:39.679
and causes the
thing to recompute,

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:41.540
that the selection changes.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.330
We could put a seed in
here if we wanted to

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:49.130
and then the selection would
be stable and would go on.

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:51.715
Every time we re-evaluate
it, we'd get the same thing.

00:34:51.715 --> 00:34:53.090
Depending on what
you want to do,

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:55.889
you want one or
the other of those.

00:34:55.889 --> 00:34:56.389
All right.

00:34:56.389 --> 00:34:58.370
Another thing we can
do with this or that we

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:00.590
have in the query
notation is macros.

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:04.100
And those can be
used to structure

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:05.930
the way you build a query.

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:10.490
So here we've got a macro called
container metric name, which

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:12.020
takes one param--

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:14.270
data metric, which
contains one parameter,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:18.320
and fetches a container
and a metric name that's

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:21.800
constructed from the name,
but starts with the Kubernetes

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:23.120
container thing.

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:25.727
Another that just--
another macro that

00:35:25.727 --> 00:35:28.310
does a table operation that just
does the filtering that we've

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:32.030
seen before and, a third,
an expression macro that,

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:35.300
basically, does the regular
expression extraction we'd

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:36.800
also seen before.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:40.838
And now I can express
the that query--

00:35:40.838 --> 00:35:43.130
and we've seen this query
before-- but I can express it

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.940
in a much more compact way.

00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:50.390
And I think at that point,
we're ready to go back

00:35:50.390 --> 00:35:53.910
to the presentation.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:57.710
And all right-- I'd say,
but wait there's more,

00:35:57.710 --> 00:35:59.510
the problem is I
think for most--

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:02.780
we've covered most of this
stuff now in the demos--

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:06.260
time shifting,
alerting, metadata,

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:07.190
and filter operations.

00:36:07.190 --> 00:36:08.900
One thing we haven't
done is talked

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:11.690
about alignment and time.

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:15.890
You would have
noticed in the group

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:22.310
by example, where I showed
the aggregation computation

00:36:22.310 --> 00:36:24.110
marching through
the time series,

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:26.210
that all the time series
timestamps lined up

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:27.320
very nicely.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:31.520
This typically doesn't
happen automatically.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:33.050
Or, I mean, it doesn't happen--

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:35.990
isn't naturally the case when
we're collecting metrics.

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:40.170
They tend to be
spread out over time.

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:44.810
And so what has to happen
is a alignment operation

00:36:44.810 --> 00:36:50.450
that takes the time series in
a table and lines up the time.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:55.920
Things that need that
like group by and join--

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:58.450
need that alignment--
will do it automatically,

00:36:58.450 --> 00:37:00.270
but you can also do
it explicitly using

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:02.010
an align operation.

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:05.250
Rate allows you to
change from deltas

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:08.820
to streams to rates
from things that

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:11.850
are absolute changes
to changes in time.

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:15.780
And every allows you to control
the period of evaluation

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:16.740
of the queries.

00:37:16.740 --> 00:37:25.800
All of the queries we've seen,
up until now, have used about--

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:28.560
a default one minute
evaluation period.

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:31.030
And actually there is
something I want to show you,

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:34.940
if we could go back to
the demo one more time.

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:42.453
Let's go back to that--
sorry JD, I threw a--

00:37:42.453 --> 00:37:44.120
JD VELASQUEZ: Where
do you want to show?

00:37:44.120 --> 00:37:44.960
JOHN BANNING: Put--

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:46.250
JD VELASQUEZ: The [INAUDIBLE]?

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:48.440
JOHN BANNING: Well, that's fine.

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:51.830
Now, you see here that
this shows one minute

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:55.560
interval, which is the interval
of what's showing on the graph.

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:59.390
But if we go to a
week, for example,

00:37:59.390 --> 00:38:01.030
we see that the interval--

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:02.440
you have to get--

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:04.930
we see the interval in
the graph is an hour.

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:08.360
So each vertical slice
of the graph is an hour.

00:38:08.360 --> 00:38:13.630
Now, we're still executing
the query once every minute

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:17.170
and what you see here,
in fact, if JD would--

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:21.070
if you'll just mouse over the--

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:24.370
something in the-- there we go--

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:27.250
you'll see is that there's
a central line, which

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:31.030
is the average value that
we computed over that hour

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:33.150
and then the range of values--

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.410
one minute values that
we saw in the hour.

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:41.380
So you can-- as time gets longer
and the graph less resolute,

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:45.670
we don't lose the spikiness
of the underlying data.

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:48.910
And we could, if we wanted
to go over to the graph

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:56.200
here and add a vertical
bar every one hour.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:57.200
JD VELASQUEZ: To the--

00:38:57.200 --> 00:38:59.450
JOHN BANNING: I'm completely
off the script now.

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:01.620
JD's probably going to kill me.

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:03.287
JD VELASQUEZ: What
you want to do, John?

00:39:03.287 --> 00:39:04.310
JOHN BANNING: Align.

00:39:04.310 --> 00:39:05.920
There should be align there.

00:39:05.920 --> 00:39:07.720
Oh, I'm sorry, every.

